00:03:42.318 --> 00:03:46.359
&gt;&gt; I think I might add also for
those of you who may have joined us for

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:47.350
other events,

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:52.776
you are probably aware of the criteria we
use when we're looking at an application.

00:03:52.776 --> 00:03:56.942
And whether it's intellectual vitality or
demonstrated leadership or

00:03:56.942 --> 00:04:01.188
even just getting to know you
the personal qualities and contributions.

00:04:01.188 --> 00:04:06.386
We see a lot of examples of that and the
letters of reference, so when you think,

00:04:06.386 --> 00:04:11.135
what is a letter of reference,
what makes a strong letter of reference?

00:04:11.135 --> 00:04:13.799
Like Alison said,
we learn a lot about you,

00:04:13.799 --> 00:04:17.833
hearing from other people and
obviously the letter of reference,

00:04:17.833 --> 00:04:21.738
both of them that you would submit,
play a pivotal role in that.

00:04:21.738 --> 00:04:26.696
And they give us some really tangible
examples of the things that we're looking

00:04:26.696 --> 00:04:30.848
for especially a lot of times
demonstrated leadership comes out.

00:04:30.848 --> 00:04:34.906
We're asking the people who write your
letter of reference to tell us what have

00:04:34.906 --> 00:04:36.635
you done and how have you done it.

00:04:36.635 --> 00:04:40.643
And that really helps us
understand you as a person and

00:04:40.643 --> 00:04:45.465
how you approach it problem solving and
how you get things done.

00:04:45.465 --> 00:04:50.167
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I really appreciate Elisa
how you mentioned evidence and

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:54.870
examples, I think,
applying to business school in particular

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:59.238
letters of reference take on
a slightly different spirit.

00:04:59.238 --> 00:05:01.748
Maybe that if you were applying to, for

00:05:01.748 --> 00:05:07.151
those of you who may remember applying to
college, those tended to be very general,

00:05:07.151 --> 00:05:11.813
if you're applying to maybe an academic or
PhD based graduate program.

00:05:11.813 --> 00:05:16.481
It's going to be much more focused on
how does this person kind of consume and

00:05:16.481 --> 00:05:20.702
kind of produce knowledge,
how do they absorb and share, right?

00:05:20.702 --> 00:05:25.371
For the business school context, that is
one element that might be shared in there,

00:05:25.371 --> 00:05:28.530
but it's not evidence,
how have you performed?

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:29.426
How have you,

00:05:29.426 --> 00:05:34.224
what kind of presence have you had in
especially organizational settings?

00:05:34.224 --> 00:05:38.382
That word impact will come up a lot,
like what's the impact you've had,

00:05:38.382 --> 00:05:41.012
what kind of footprints
have you left behind?

00:05:41.012 --> 00:05:46.001
So, I think over the course of today,
unpacking that will be helpful because

00:05:46.001 --> 00:05:50.915
there is a slight different nuance to
letters of ref for business school than

00:05:50.915 --> 00:05:55.470
I've seen, even having worked in
other admissions offices before.

00:05:56.830 --> 00:06:01.044
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely and I think your
answers also give us insight into what

00:06:01.044 --> 00:06:05.885
makes a strong letter recommendation and
I think there should be like a underline,

00:06:05.885 --> 00:06:10.110
double underline, highlight,
bolded around evidence.

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:13.849
I think throughout the application
process, we're not just looking for what

00:06:13.849 --> 00:06:17.826
you've accomplished, but we're interested
in how you have accomplished that.

00:06:17.826 --> 00:06:22.800
And as Alison said, hearing other voices
in your application speak to not just what

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.295
you've accomplished, but
how, can be really helpful.

00:06:26.295 --> 00:06:31.688
How have you demonstrated leadership
are you able to, creatively problem solve,

00:06:31.688 --> 00:06:35.934
you take what's given to you and
see beyond what's given to you?

00:06:35.934 --> 00:06:39.682
Do you work beyond the expectations
that are given to you?

00:06:39.682 --> 00:06:44.225
Do you make your workplaces more
inclusive, are you able to mobilize and

00:06:44.225 --> 00:06:47.737
influence other people to get
on board with your ideas?

00:06:47.737 --> 00:06:51.670
Those are all things that are really
helpful for us when we think about your

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:55.922
leadership potential, when we think about
some of your personal qualities and

00:06:55.922 --> 00:06:59.423
contributions to the organizations
that you've been a part of.

00:06:59.423 --> 00:07:04.676
So, thank you all for that introduction
to letters of recommendation,

00:07:04.676 --> 00:07:08.981
I'm going to ask a couple more
of our prepared questions and

00:07:08.981 --> 00:07:11.410
then turn it over to the Q&amp;A box.

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:12.931
So, for our attendees,

00:07:12.931 --> 00:07:17.279
please continue submitting your
amazing and thoughtful questions and

00:07:17.279 --> 00:07:22.220
please continue uploading questions
that you're curious to learn more about.

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:28.838
I'm also wondering if you all,
our panelists have any tips for

00:07:28.838 --> 00:07:32.990
helping set your recommenders up for
success?

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:39.203
I think obviously writing a letter of
reference for someone, it takes time,

00:07:39.203 --> 00:07:44.269
it takes energy, how do applicants
make sure that their letter

00:07:44.269 --> 00:07:50.590
writers are set up for success and
to be the best advocates possible?

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:55.488
&gt;&gt; I can start us off, I think the most
important thing is give them plenty

00:07:55.488 --> 00:08:00.471
of warning, plenty of time,
probably how busy your recommender is and

00:08:00.471 --> 00:08:05.630
I would say take them out to coffee or
tea or virtual coffee or tea and

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:11.113
Help them understand what the timeline
is and what the questions are.

00:08:11.113 --> 00:08:15.647
You can also remind them
of maybe some specific

00:08:15.647 --> 00:08:19.723
examples that they might want to include.

00:08:19.723 --> 00:08:22.179
As we said earlier,
evidence is so important.

00:08:22.179 --> 00:08:26.833
So it's those specifics anecdotes,
details, examples.

00:08:26.833 --> 00:08:29.400
So you want to be sure to remind them to

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:33.431
support whatever their
assertions are with examples.

00:08:33.431 --> 00:08:38.449
You would be surprised that we
don't often get really negative

00:08:38.449 --> 00:08:43.574
recommendations where somebody
is being completely trashed.

00:08:43.574 --> 00:08:46.183
That doesn't happen very often.

00:08:46.183 --> 00:08:51.323
But more often than we would like, we get
recommendations where they might say,

00:08:51.323 --> 00:08:55.042
Jessica is the best person we
hired in the last five years.

00:08:55.042 --> 00:08:59.195
Period, end of sentence, end of answer.

00:08:59.195 --> 00:09:03.625
No examples of what made
Jessica such an amazing hire.

00:09:03.625 --> 00:09:08.252
So you want to make sure
that they include examples.

00:09:08.252 --> 00:09:10.374
&gt;&gt; And that was a great example.

00:09:10.374 --> 00:09:14.324
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:09:14.324 --> 00:09:19.301
I would also say that for many, we just
talked about how the business school

00:09:19.301 --> 00:09:21.992
letter of rec is a little bit different.

00:09:21.992 --> 00:09:26.632
So we're going to be asking all of our
recommenders the same two questions that

00:09:26.632 --> 00:09:28.179
are along the lines like,

00:09:28.179 --> 00:09:31.782
how was this person performed
relative to qualified peers.

00:09:31.782 --> 00:09:34.842
Other is,
it tells about a time it was feedback.

00:09:34.842 --> 00:09:39.672
So even sharing those questions and
talking through and understanding, okay,

00:09:39.672 --> 00:09:44.873
here's a great structure, they're actually
asking for what they're looking for.

00:09:44.873 --> 00:09:49.805
And then the other thing you can
share with them is the evaluation

00:09:49.805 --> 00:09:51.363
criteria website.

00:09:51.363 --> 00:09:54.510
We just talked a lot about
intellectual vitality,

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:59.209
demonstrated leadership potential,
but those are kind of squishy words.

00:09:59.209 --> 00:10:04.186
And they sound very high level, but
on that website, it actually brings it

00:10:04.186 --> 00:10:08.689
down to tangible bite size things
that as they're thinking about,

00:10:08.689 --> 00:10:11.612
hey, here are the things Jessica has done.

00:10:11.612 --> 00:10:12.412
She's great.

00:10:12.412 --> 00:10:16.938
I didn't realize that falls into this
bucket of what they're looking for.

00:10:16.938 --> 00:10:19.287
And so that can also help cue them.

00:10:19.287 --> 00:10:22.807
And so the types of examples that they
could use to support their assertions.

00:10:25.348 --> 00:10:25.997
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:10:25.997 --> 00:10:31.605
And that's actually a great segue to
one of the questions in the Q&amp;A box and

00:10:31.605 --> 00:10:37.047
Elisa, if you have more to add,
I definitely want to make space for that.

00:10:37.047 --> 00:10:40.512
But Cornelia in the Q&amp; Abox asked if
there's a package we can share with

00:10:40.512 --> 00:10:43.658
references to give them a sense
of what Stanford is looking for.

00:10:43.658 --> 00:10:49.067
I'm going to drop a link in the chats to
more information on letters of reference.

00:10:49.067 --> 00:10:52.931
And if you scroll towards the bottom,

00:10:52.931 --> 00:10:57.639
you can see some of
the questions that we ask and

00:10:57.639 --> 00:11:01.525
more information for recommenders.

00:11:01.525 --> 00:11:03.218
So I will drop that in the chat.

00:11:03.218 --> 00:11:07.854
Elisa, do you have any other tips for
setting up recommendation writers for

00:11:07.854 --> 00:11:08.513
success?

00:11:08.513 --> 00:11:09.244
&gt;&gt; No.

00:11:09.244 --> 00:11:12.175
I mean, I think well and
Allison touched on the key things and

00:11:12.175 --> 00:11:16.161
I would just echo especially what Allison
said at the beginning to give them time.

00:11:16.161 --> 00:11:20.697
One it's just a sign of respect
that you're giving them the time,

00:11:20.697 --> 00:11:24.828
you're asking for them to take
that time to be thoughtful and

00:11:24.828 --> 00:11:28.879
provide specifics that will
help you in your application.

00:11:28.879 --> 00:11:29.466
But also,

00:11:29.466 --> 00:11:33.516
as many of you may know about any
application process to business school,

00:11:33.516 --> 00:11:37.850
but especially the Stanford application,
it's an introspective process.

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:43.130
We ask lots of questions, not only to you,
but to the recommenders also.

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:49.127
And again, just the more time someone can
spend on that, to provide those specific

00:11:49.127 --> 00:11:54.964
concrete examples that really help us
understand who you are, is a great thing.

00:11:54.964 --> 00:11:58.450
So that was already said,
and I just echo that.

00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:00.725
&gt;&gt; Jessica if I just
could add one more thing.

00:12:00.725 --> 00:12:04.664
When you're asking them to be
your recommender, and again,

00:12:04.664 --> 00:12:09.590
these are probably people you have
mentoring relationships anyway with, so

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:12.941
presumably, you can have
an honest conversation.

00:12:12.941 --> 00:12:18.213
If you detect any reluctance,
let them off the hook.

00:12:18.213 --> 00:12:23.828
I think the reason we sometimes
get those one sentence

00:12:23.828 --> 00:12:29.961
answers is somebody didn't
want to turn down the request.

00:12:29.961 --> 00:12:32.703
That they honestly really don't
have time and they feel rushed.

00:12:32.703 --> 00:12:36.996
And so you want to make sure that
not only they know you well, but

00:12:36.996 --> 00:12:41.868
they're In a position to have the time
to write a thoughtful letter and

00:12:41.868 --> 00:12:44.195
are excited to be your advocate.

00:12:44.195 --> 00:12:48.886
&gt;&gt; I would also add,
especially if there are folks who might

00:12:48.886 --> 00:12:53.000
be writing recommendations
watching right now.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:57.910
The way we look at the recommendation,
it's for those evidence pieces in context.

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:01.402
So it doesn't necessarily mean
you have to be versed in,

00:13:01.402 --> 00:13:03.603
quote unquote business language.

00:13:03.603 --> 00:13:06.840
It also doesn't have to
be the most beautiful.

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:10.427
It doesn't have to be
artistic writing at all,

00:13:10.427 --> 00:13:14.208
even sometimes factual,
straight to the point.

00:13:14.208 --> 00:13:16.754
Here's the impact this person made.

00:13:16.754 --> 00:13:18.420
Here's the scope of it.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:19.569
Here's the scale of it.

00:13:19.569 --> 00:13:20.921
Here's what it looks like.

00:13:20.921 --> 00:13:23.786
Is this normal for
something at this individual's level?

00:13:23.786 --> 00:13:25.918
Have they gone above and beyond?

00:13:25.918 --> 00:13:27.363
And here are the reasons why.

00:13:27.363 --> 00:13:29.414
And now here are the ripple effects of it.

00:13:29.414 --> 00:13:33.558
Sometimes getting straight to that
point is where we find value and

00:13:33.558 --> 00:13:37.042
hopefully it helps you save
a little bit of time as well.

00:13:37.042 --> 00:13:41.695
I know this can also be a concern for
folks whose

00:13:41.695 --> 00:13:46.716
language of writing
proficiency is not English.

00:13:46.716 --> 00:13:50.832
And so again, it's not about
kind of how it's written, but

00:13:50.832 --> 00:13:53.721
what's written in there in the content.

00:13:53.721 --> 00:13:58.623
So know that we are trained and
we work really hard to make sure we're

00:13:58.623 --> 00:14:02.751
trying to suss that stuff out and
let the evidence shine.

00:14:02.751 --> 00:14:04.570
So just wanted to also put that out there.

00:14:04.570 --> 00:14:08.478
I know there's some folks who can be
a little hesitant because they're not sure

00:14:08.478 --> 00:14:11.758
what a business school recommendation
looks like or how we use it.

00:14:11.758 --> 00:14:13.936
&gt;&gt; And these letters are not long.

00:14:13.936 --> 00:14:18.851
Correct me if I'm wrong, but typically
the letters are two to four pages,

00:14:18.851 --> 00:14:20.888
would you say at least on will.

00:14:20.888 --> 00:14:22.581
They're not long, long letters.

00:14:22.581 --> 00:14:27.253
And maybe we should address
if your recommender is

00:14:27.253 --> 00:14:31.935
not able to write in English,
what you should do.

00:14:36.922 --> 00:14:39.470
&gt;&gt; I mean, [LAUGH] that's a good team.

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:42.621
I think we're jumping right
in some granular thing, so

00:14:42.621 --> 00:14:44.640
we can definitely talk about that.

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:49.546
If the primary language of whoever might
be writing your letter of reference is not

00:14:49.546 --> 00:14:54.041
English, they could write in the language
they're most comfortable in.

00:14:54.041 --> 00:14:58.471
But then we do ask that it be
translated into English upon submitting

00:14:58.471 --> 00:15:00.188
the letter of reference.

00:15:00.188 --> 00:15:04.700
And again, Will said,
perfect grammar, syntax, and

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:07.875
layout is not what we're looking for.

00:15:07.875 --> 00:15:13.209
We're looking for what they're going to
share about you as a person, as someone

00:15:13.209 --> 00:15:18.479
who worked in their organization, as
someone who had impact, what did you do?

00:15:18.479 --> 00:15:19.646
How did you do it?

00:15:19.646 --> 00:15:23.557
What was the ripple effect,
like Will sad, of what you've done?

00:15:23.557 --> 00:15:26.706
And that can come through
in a translation, again,

00:15:26.706 --> 00:15:29.005
even if their grammar is not perfect.

00:15:29.005 --> 00:15:34.176
So for those who are thinking about asking
someone whose primary language is not

00:15:34.176 --> 00:15:39.517
English, please don't worry about how
the translation might come across to us.

00:15:39.517 --> 00:15:45.107
It doesn't need to be perfect grammar for
us to be able, like Will sad, to see

00:15:45.107 --> 00:15:50.794
those examples and to understand the
context of the impact that you've made.

00:15:50.794 --> 00:15:53.706
So that is something we do ask for.

00:15:53.706 --> 00:16:00.472
And I think this is leading us into, we've
talked about, Allison had great advice.

00:16:00.472 --> 00:16:03.210
If you say to someone, hey,
I'm applying to business school,

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:04.898
can you write me a letter of reference?

00:16:04.898 --> 00:16:09.877
And you sense hesitation, That's a good,
good first hint to maybe ask someone else.

00:16:09.877 --> 00:16:12.763
But I think we should step back and
talk about,

00:16:12.763 --> 00:16:16.464
who do we want these letters of
reference to be from and why?

00:16:16.464 --> 00:16:22.715
So I think that many of you probably
know if you've started an application or

00:16:22.715 --> 00:16:28.198
looked on our web pages that we do ask for
two letters of reference.

00:16:28.198 --> 00:16:31.779
And we ask that they be from people
who have supervised your work.

00:16:31.779 --> 00:16:36.036
And we specifically asked that the first
one be from someone currently supervising

00:16:36.036 --> 00:16:36.657
your work.

00:16:36.657 --> 00:16:40.791
And I do see a lot of
questions out there around.

00:16:40.791 --> 00:16:44.557
I maybe had worked for someone for
four years and I just switched jobs, and

00:16:44.557 --> 00:16:47.860
I'm with someone for only six weeks now,
and things like that.

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:49.590
So we can talk about exceptions to this.

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:51.098
But in general, we ask that for

00:16:51.098 --> 00:16:54.123
the first time to be from someone
currently supervising you.

00:16:54.123 --> 00:16:57.878
Again back to that criteria
that we're looking for.

00:16:57.878 --> 00:17:02.088
Someone who's currently supervising
you is going to be able to tell us,

00:17:02.088 --> 00:17:04.580
what have you done and
how have you done it?

00:17:04.580 --> 00:17:08.893
What is the impact that you're making in
your current role, in your current job,

00:17:08.893 --> 00:17:11.580
in your current organization?

00:17:11.580 --> 00:17:16.037
Are you taking advantage of
opportunities presented to you.

00:17:16.037 --> 00:17:18.096
Are you going above and
beyond, all of those things.

00:17:18.096 --> 00:17:22.260
And we'll get a great sense of that
from someone who currently supervises

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:23.140
your work.

00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:29.743
There's a lot of times we generally give
advice around the questions, what do I do?

00:17:29.743 --> 00:17:32.501
I just started working with someone
they don't know me that well.

00:17:32.501 --> 00:17:35.384
Use your best judgment,
kind of like what Allison was hinting at.

00:17:35.384 --> 00:17:39.869
If someone's hesitant when you
ask them to be your recommender.

00:17:39.869 --> 00:17:44.435
Same thing here in the sense that you want
to make sure that when you really think

00:17:44.435 --> 00:17:47.280
about what is this person
going to be able to say.

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:51.161
And do they know me well enough, that
you're asking the right person, right?

00:17:51.161 --> 00:17:52.600
And so you can use your best judgment.

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:55.581
If you have been working for
someone for a short time period, or

00:17:55.581 --> 00:17:58.968
perhaps you're working for your mom or
dad, it's a family business.

00:17:58.968 --> 00:18:02.780
Or perhaps you're a founder
of a company yourself.

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:07.762
And so there isn't someone that
supervises you in a traditional way,

00:18:07.762 --> 00:18:10.160
then use your best judgment.

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:14.095
Who's going to be able to provide
those concrete examples to be

00:18:14.095 --> 00:18:15.650
a member of your board.

00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:17.640
It could be someone else
that you work with.

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:21.299
It could be the person you previously
worked for since you're only working for

00:18:21.299 --> 00:18:22.850
someone for a few weeks.

00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:26.267
So again,
think about what we're looking for.

00:18:26.267 --> 00:18:30.520
Ultimately, this has to sit
really well inside of you.

00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:33.329
We always say the best time to apply is
when you feel like your application is

00:18:33.329 --> 00:18:34.010
at its best.

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:36.762
And we would hate for
you to have submit your application and

00:18:36.762 --> 00:18:40.410
then wish you had asked someone else,
or had a different letter of reference.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:43.740
So again, use your best judgment
in terms of who you ask.

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:45.715
But there are a lot of
reasons why we asked for

00:18:45.715 --> 00:18:47.539
one to be from your current supervisor.

00:18:47.539 --> 00:18:49.571
And I'm going to pass a little,
because I'm talking a lot.

00:18:49.571 --> 00:18:54.896
So, I don't know whether Allison
have anything else to add?

00:18:54.896 --> 00:18:56.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think Elisa, you're right.

00:18:56.330 --> 00:19:00.600
A lot of the questions that are coming up
in the Q&amp;A box are about how to choose

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:01.753
your recommender.

00:19:01.753 --> 00:19:05.328
And I appreciate that you went
over what exactly do we ask for?

00:19:05.328 --> 00:19:09.813
So two letters of reference,
one from a current direct supervisor or

00:19:09.813 --> 00:19:11.449
next best alternative.

00:19:11.449 --> 00:19:15.585
Because we appreciate that there are,
as Elisa said, many situations in which

00:19:15.585 --> 00:19:20.530
you're not able to ask your current direct
supervisor for a letter of reference.

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:25.010
And then another from somebody else who
has supervised your work in some capacity.

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:30.510
But in addition to that,
do you all have any advice for

00:19:30.510 --> 00:19:32.700
how to choose a recommender?

00:19:34.360 --> 00:19:36.380
And there are several
questions on this topic.

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:41.820
I think one that has sort of risen to
the top in the Q&amp;A box is from Konica.

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:45.950
Who, I think this is coming from
recognizing that our process is holistic.

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:50.360
That the letters of recommendation are one
of many pieces of your application.

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:55.753
So Konica asks,
do you prefer that similar characteristics

00:19:55.753 --> 00:20:01.156
about a candidate or
highlighted in both recommendations?

00:20:01.156 --> 00:20:02.027
Are you looking for

00:20:02.027 --> 00:20:05.090
different characteristics
highlighted from each recommender?

00:20:07.530 --> 00:20:11.222
So, I think again,
kind of asking the question like,

00:20:11.222 --> 00:20:16.460
how do you choose your recommenders and
beyond evidence, how do you decide?

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:22.370
How do you think about what story
the recommendation should tell?

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:27.341
And how should it contribute to
the overall story of your application?

00:20:27.341 --> 00:20:27.888
Yeah.

00:20:27.888 --> 00:20:29.960
&gt;&gt; First of all we have no preference.

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:34.685
And it's really up to you
how you present yourself.

00:20:34.685 --> 00:20:39.170
The questions to the recommenders
are on our website so you can see them.

00:20:39.170 --> 00:20:43.944
And I would say, be asking yourself,
who can best answer these questions?

00:20:43.944 --> 00:20:47.242
And if you're lucky enough
to have multiple people,

00:20:47.242 --> 00:20:50.614
maybe there's two that have
more robust examples, or

00:20:50.614 --> 00:20:54.200
more examples that they
can include in the letter.

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:58.048
And as far as current
versus previous jobs,

00:20:58.048 --> 00:21:03.049
I think that's really how you
want to present your story.

00:21:03.049 --> 00:21:03.609
I mean,

00:21:03.609 --> 00:21:08.729
if you want to show perhaps growth over
time in terms of your own development,

00:21:08.729 --> 00:21:13.700
maybe you want to get a previous
supervisor and then a current supervisor.

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:18.666
If you want to really dig deep into
current impact and accomplishments,

00:21:18.666 --> 00:21:22.400
maybe you want two people
from your current company.

00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:28.185
And we've certainly seen excellent
recommendations from both scenarios.

00:21:28.185 --> 00:21:31.074
And I'm sure there are other
ways to think about it.

00:21:31.074 --> 00:21:36.649
But it's really up to you how you
want to present your narrative.

00:21:36.649 --> 00:21:40.851
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I would even make it easier than that.

00:21:40.851 --> 00:21:46.078
Sometimes trying to over strategize
might have you trying to find

00:21:46.078 --> 00:21:52.649
different people to fit this perfect image
of who you want to present yourself as.

00:21:52.649 --> 00:21:55.013
Quinn, one way I think
about it as well is,

00:21:55.013 --> 00:21:59.028
sometimes the people who are providing
those perspectives as Alison and

00:21:59.028 --> 00:22:02.540
Elisa were talking about,
they're your champions already.

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:06.813
These are the folks who might
be your mentors, advocates, and

00:22:06.813 --> 00:22:09.806
sponsors in different facets of your life.

00:22:09.806 --> 00:22:13.884
And you'd be surprised at how many people
even within the same organization,

00:22:13.884 --> 00:22:18.048
maybe because they're on different teams,
or they're at different levels.

00:22:18.048 --> 00:22:20.080
Or if they've worked with
you on different projects.

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:24.630
Even in those instances, they might have
different understandings of how you've

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:27.690
operated and
have had different experiences with you.

00:22:29.150 --> 00:22:32.346
And so it really does come back to what
Allison was saying at the beginning.

00:22:32.346 --> 00:22:35.935
It's how have other
people been impacted by,

00:22:35.935 --> 00:22:39.165
and what was their experience with that?

00:22:39.165 --> 00:22:41.553
How can they communicate that to us.

00:22:41.553 --> 00:22:43.982
And everybody will do that
a little bit differently.

00:22:43.982 --> 00:22:48.620
And we definitely trust you to figure out
who is going to do that best for you.

00:22:48.620 --> 00:22:51.392
You may want to strategize if
that becomes overwhelming,

00:22:51.392 --> 00:22:52.900
because I know sometimes it can be.

00:22:54.030 --> 00:22:57.430
This is going to sound weird because
it's like the one like trust the unit,

00:22:57.430 --> 00:22:59.708
like trust your instincts, trust your gut.

00:22:59.708 --> 00:23:04.682
If something says, I think this is someone
who can really advocate for me and

00:23:04.682 --> 00:23:06.547
it feels like a good choice,

00:23:06.547 --> 00:23:11.310
go with that one regardless of their
relationship to your other writer.

00:23:15.005 --> 00:23:20.015
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, Elisa,
anything you would want to add here?

00:23:20.015 --> 00:23:21.590
&gt;&gt; I think we've covered that really well.

00:23:21.590 --> 00:23:24.438
I don't have anything to
add specifically to that.

00:23:24.438 --> 00:23:29.585
&gt;&gt; Awesome, looking in the Q&amp;A box again,

00:23:29.585 --> 00:23:36.360
and there are a couple
questions about timing as well.

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:39.972
So on the letters of reference
page on our website,

00:23:39.972 --> 00:23:45.602
we suggest asking somebody who has worked
with you within the last three years.

00:23:45.602 --> 00:23:50.013
And there are a couple
questions about timing,

00:23:50.013 --> 00:23:55.317
whether it's okay to ask
somebody from a previous role.

00:23:55.317 --> 00:23:59.246
Even going beyond three years ago,
say five years ago,

00:23:59.246 --> 00:24:03.844
do you all have any advice on sort
of figuring out the timing of your

00:24:03.844 --> 00:24:07.640
relationship with your
letters of recommenders?

00:24:07.640 --> 00:24:08.550
Any advice there?

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:15.742
I think it was mentioned earlier, kind of
what you've done in the past as a really

00:24:15.742 --> 00:24:20.470
good predictor of how you might approach
problems or lead in the future.

00:24:20.470 --> 00:24:24.414
And so when we asked that one of the
letters of reference be from someone who

00:24:24.414 --> 00:24:27.650
has worked with you in
the past three years.

00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:31.070
That's because we're looking for
current or

00:24:31.070 --> 00:24:36.250
more recent examples of what have you
done and and how have you done it?

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:38.140
What has your impact been?

00:24:38.140 --> 00:24:43.320
That said earlier I also said,
use your best judgement, so for

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:50.092
instance, if you're going to, maybe choose
someone who is from four years ago.

00:24:50.092 --> 00:24:54.947
Or like I said, you're choosing someone
who's not your direct supervisor for

00:24:54.947 --> 00:24:59.790
one of those reasons we cited, you do
have the chance to explain to us why.

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:03.758
And that would provide context for
us if you don't, but

00:25:03.758 --> 00:25:09.514
there's definitely a reason why we asked
that it be within the last three years.

00:25:09.514 --> 00:25:13.723
Again, looking for that more recent
examples of what you've done, and

00:25:13.723 --> 00:25:18.215
we're looking for it specifically in
the job you've most recently been in.

00:25:19.355 --> 00:25:25.055
But again, you have to use your best
judgment about who you're asking.

00:25:25.055 --> 00:25:28.151
I saw I have a question to you around for
instance,

00:25:28.151 --> 00:25:31.689
maybe you have been at a company for
a really long time, so

00:25:31.689 --> 00:25:37.100
you're going to choose two people from the
same company and that's absolutely fine.

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:42.125
So someone's asking that if those
are the two people who can really provide

00:25:42.125 --> 00:25:46.830
the best examples again of what you've
done and know you the best out of

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:52.320
everyone you might be thinking about
asking for a letter of reference.

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:57.110
Then again, if that feels right to you,
that's absolutely fine.

00:25:57.110 --> 00:26:01.253
But like we said, you could also choose
your second one to be from someone else

00:26:01.253 --> 00:26:05.570
who has supervised you in some way, it
could be somewhere else you use to work.

00:26:05.570 --> 00:26:10.082
It could be that someone has
supervised you in an activity

00:26:10.082 --> 00:26:12.740
outside of work in some way.

00:26:12.740 --> 00:26:17.193
So again, this is really about
you thinking through all

00:26:17.193 --> 00:26:20.795
the people you're
thinking about asking and

00:26:20.795 --> 00:26:26.305
who might be able to provide those
examples in the best light for you.

00:26:26.305 --> 00:26:32.106
&gt;&gt; Those of you who are in client services
business, businesses client might

00:26:32.106 --> 00:26:37.460
be really good choice, so
doesn't have to be a current supervisor.

00:26:38.740 --> 00:26:42.366
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'll add one wrinkle to this for
folks who might be reapplying,

00:26:42.366 --> 00:26:46.850
you might have asked recommenders before,
you might be asking the same recommenders.

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:50.865
Again, trust your instincts, we have no
preference, if you choose the same people,

00:26:50.865 --> 00:26:53.505
you choose different people
because maybe the relationships

00:26:53.505 --> 00:26:55.450
in your life have changed.

00:26:55.450 --> 00:27:00.045
If you are asking the same people, I
definitely recommend you ask them to write

00:27:00.045 --> 00:27:04.220
the letter again from fresh eyes,
even though it might be the exact same

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:08.280
questions, their relationship
the things they have seen you done.

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:12.119
Since you last applied might
have additional information or

00:27:12.119 --> 00:27:16.409
ways that they now understand your
impact on in your organization or

00:27:16.409 --> 00:27:18.990
whatever setting you were in.

00:27:18.990 --> 00:27:22.679
So I think that's one thing I
would definitely recommend,

00:27:22.679 --> 00:27:27.103
at least in terms of time recency,
just have them come with fresh eyes,

00:27:27.103 --> 00:27:29.780
which would, that's what we're doing.

00:27:29.780 --> 00:27:33.088
We're trying to look at who
you are in the here and now.

00:27:33.088 --> 00:27:39.750
&gt;&gt; That's great advice, thanks, we also
have a question about deferred admission.

00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:44.677
Would you all be able to speak to sort
of the differences between academic

00:27:44.677 --> 00:27:46.712
letters of recommendation.

00:27:46.712 --> 00:27:50.012
Professional letters of recommendation,
and any tips or

00:27:50.012 --> 00:27:53.377
differences that you would want
attendees to keep in mind,

00:27:53.377 --> 00:27:56.559
as they sort of navigate their
choices between the two?

00:27:59.583 --> 00:28:04.910
&gt;&gt; For deferred admits, so specifically
people who might be at currently

00:28:04.910 --> 00:28:10.776
a full time student, who are thinking
about applying to the GSB We evaluate

00:28:10.776 --> 00:28:15.820
each application with the same criteria
that I mentioned at the beginning.

00:28:15.820 --> 00:28:18.703
So, demonstrated leadership
potential intellectual vitality,

00:28:18.703 --> 00:28:20.530
personal qualities and contributions.

00:28:20.530 --> 00:28:25.457
So, we suggest that you start
select recommenders then who in

00:28:25.457 --> 00:28:27.933
some way have supervised you.

00:28:27.933 --> 00:28:32.311
Knowing that you don't have years of work
experience like someone who has already

00:28:32.311 --> 00:28:35.230
graduated from undergrad and
worked for a few years but

00:28:35.230 --> 00:28:38.929
perhaps someone has supervised
your extracurricular activities.

00:28:38.929 --> 00:28:43.918
Or perhaps you've had a part time
internship or even a part time job,

00:28:43.918 --> 00:28:48.060
summer internship,
job through your years of college.

00:28:49.080 --> 00:28:53.578
There's lots of things that you have
probably been involved in as a full time

00:28:53.578 --> 00:28:58.370
student that demonstrate leadership where
you've been able to have an impact.

00:28:58.370 --> 00:28:59.450
And so, again,

00:28:59.450 --> 00:29:04.700
in those roles has there been someone
supervising you in some capacity?

00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:08.826
That might be a good person to think about
asking for your letter of reference,

00:29:08.826 --> 00:29:12.282
this could be an academic letter
of reference then in that sense.

00:29:12.282 --> 00:29:15.736
Perhaps you've been doing research for
a professor, and

00:29:15.736 --> 00:29:20.598
they know you very well and they're able
to speak to the impact that you've had and

00:29:20.598 --> 00:29:23.680
what you've done in working with them.

00:29:23.680 --> 00:29:27.651
So that's perfectly acceptable to, but
also we encourage you just to think

00:29:27.651 --> 00:29:31.809
about extra curricular activities you
may have been involved in internships,

00:29:31.809 --> 00:29:32.960
part time work.

00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:37.031
All of those are good places to start
thinking about asking someone for

00:29:37.031 --> 00:29:38.413
a letter of reference.

00:29:38.413 --> 00:29:42.369
&gt;&gt; I would also tie this back to Will's
reminder from the beginning about

00:29:42.369 --> 00:29:46.360
business school recommendations
being a little bit different.

00:29:46.360 --> 00:29:50.084
So if you do ask a professor or
an academic to write a recommendation,

00:29:50.084 --> 00:29:54.390
you want to make sure they understand
they're not writing a recommendation for

00:29:54.390 --> 00:29:55.540
a PhD program.

00:29:55.540 --> 00:29:59.182
This is a management program
where we are looking for

00:29:59.182 --> 00:30:02.690
evidence of leadership potential.

00:30:02.690 --> 00:30:08.463
So again, often from professors,
we will get a letter that says,

00:30:08.463 --> 00:30:14.620
Elisa sat in the third row in the class,
she got to B plus a midterm.

00:30:14.620 --> 00:30:16.509
Here's the syllabus, and

00:30:16.509 --> 00:30:22.270
that's not really helpful to us in terms
of assessing your leadership potential.

00:30:22.270 --> 00:30:28.159
But if they're talking about impact
you've had, perhaps as a TA or

00:30:28.159 --> 00:30:35.250
in the lab or knowledge that you
created that really pushed boundaries.

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:38.958
That's the kind of thing
that can be helpful to us,

00:30:38.958 --> 00:30:42.337
nice job with that B plus
at least I heard that.

00:30:42.337 --> 00:30:47.090
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:30:47.090 --> 00:30:49.953
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you all,

00:30:49.953 --> 00:30:55.560
let me see there is another
great question in here.

00:30:57.310 --> 00:31:03.674
What advice can I give my recommenders to
best boost my chances of being accepted or

00:31:03.674 --> 00:31:07.930
to best support them in
writing a letter of reference?

00:31:07.930 --> 00:31:12.129
So we talked about setting up
recommenders for success but

00:31:12.129 --> 00:31:17.959
are there tips that applicants can give
If recommenders are feeling uncertain or

00:31:17.959 --> 00:31:23.720
are not super confident about what should
go in that letter, what can they say?

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:29.190
&gt;&gt; Maybe three things, so one,
definitely share this video with them,

00:31:29.190 --> 00:31:33.860
hopefully they'll be able to pull out some
content that will help them understand.

00:31:33.860 --> 00:31:36.723
And figure out where some
additional resources are,

00:31:36.723 --> 00:31:40.410
provide them with the website,
the evaluation criteria to help them

00:31:40.410 --> 00:31:43.590
understand the things
that we're looking for.

00:31:43.590 --> 00:31:46.907
And then my third thing would
be if they have questions,

00:31:46.907 --> 00:31:49.135
have them contact us directly.

00:31:49.135 --> 00:31:53.028
Sometimes things might get lost in that
game of telephone where they're asking you

00:31:53.028 --> 00:31:54.363
and then you're asking us,

00:31:54.363 --> 00:31:57.775
if they're able to ask us we're able
to make that connection directly.

00:31:57.775 --> 00:32:00.656
And if there's any clarification
that needs to happen,

00:32:00.656 --> 00:32:03.775
there's not going to be that
extra time lag in between.

00:32:03.775 --> 00:32:07.136
And hopefully that's one less thing you
have to do in that that realm of upward

00:32:07.136 --> 00:32:08.118
management.

00:32:08.118 --> 00:32:10.193
Which again is going to be
a natural part of this.

00:32:10.193 --> 00:32:14.942
But yeah, definitely, if they have
questions, have them reach out to us and

00:32:14.942 --> 00:32:15.977
we can help out.

00:32:20.479 --> 00:32:24.418
&gt;&gt; Awesome, Allison, Elisa,
anything you'd add tips for recommenders?

00:32:24.418 --> 00:32:25.173
&gt;&gt; Those are great tips.

00:32:25.173 --> 00:32:28.237
I don't feel like I would
add anything to tips but

00:32:28.237 --> 00:32:32.361
in general, we want them to be
authentic and true to themselves.

00:32:32.361 --> 00:32:36.777
So, this question is kind of
stemming from a place of,

00:32:36.777 --> 00:32:39.467
what do you think we want to hear?

00:32:39.467 --> 00:32:45.650
And hopefully we've been able to convey
to you today that we're looking for

00:32:45.650 --> 00:32:51.832
tangible examples of your impact and
specifics like Allison said, hearing

00:32:51.832 --> 00:32:57.934
that Will is the best person I've worked
with in five years [LAUGH] is great.

00:32:57.934 --> 00:33:01.498
That's a really positive statement, but

00:33:01.498 --> 00:33:06.151
it isn't specific in terms
of reasons why perhaps, and

00:33:06.151 --> 00:33:11.110
in terms of kind of evidence of impact and
things like that.

00:33:11.110 --> 00:33:16.132
So again, encourage them to be
as specific as possible, but

00:33:16.132 --> 00:33:21.354
more so just encourage them to
answer the questions honestly.

00:33:21.354 --> 00:33:27.023
We talked about things that we value
at the beginning, be transparent.

00:33:27.023 --> 00:33:31.559
There isn't anything,
there isn't a format, or a statement or

00:33:31.559 --> 00:33:34.124
something that we're looking for.

00:33:34.124 --> 00:33:38.611
We're looking for those examples and we're
really hoping to try to understand who you

00:33:38.611 --> 00:33:41.020
are as a person throughout
your application.

00:33:41.020 --> 00:33:45.635
And there's lots of different areas of
the application where we find that and

00:33:45.635 --> 00:33:49.045
letters of reference
are definitely one of those areas.

00:33:49.045 --> 00:33:53.442
&gt;&gt; I would just add too that I
think sometimes applicants and

00:33:53.442 --> 00:33:58.114
maybe recommenders worry about,
gee, I better not say that

00:33:58.114 --> 00:34:03.535
the candidate ever struggled with
anything or ever grew in their job.

00:34:03.535 --> 00:34:09.150
It's like we don't expect
you to spring from

00:34:09.150 --> 00:34:16.552
the womb perfectly formed in
this superlative performer.

00:34:16.552 --> 00:34:22.161
So, it can actually make a recommendation
more credible if they're

00:34:22.161 --> 00:34:28.555
honest about maybe there were areas where
you struggled in your job or things.

00:34:28.555 --> 00:34:33.180
I mean, we all have development areas, so,

00:34:33.180 --> 00:34:37.931
how you dealt with
constructive criticism and

00:34:37.931 --> 00:34:41.724
things that you may have grown from.

00:34:41.724 --> 00:34:44.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think at the heart of it,

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:49.926
it's what stood out to them about
kind of their interactions with you.

00:34:49.926 --> 00:34:54.522
And that is a very open-ended statement
because that's going to be different for

00:34:54.522 --> 00:34:55.214
everyone.

00:34:55.214 --> 00:34:59.447
And yes we talk a lot about intellectual
vitality, demonstrated leadership

00:34:59.447 --> 00:35:03.951
potential, but we are also not looking for
any particular balance within there.

00:35:03.951 --> 00:35:07.913
And so as you're talking to the folks
that you might be asking, or

00:35:07.913 --> 00:35:11.298
they're asking,
what are some things to think about,

00:35:11.298 --> 00:35:15.496
just let them know that we don't
have a preference for that balance.

00:35:15.496 --> 00:35:20.315
Focus on the things that were meaningful
to them, that stood out to them,

00:35:20.315 --> 00:35:23.747
and they feel should be
called out in support of you.

00:35:23.747 --> 00:35:27.302
Knowing that everybody's letters
are going to have a different balance of

00:35:27.302 --> 00:35:28.302
different things.

00:35:28.302 --> 00:35:32.137
No two people are going to be called
the best people that they work with for

00:35:32.137 --> 00:35:33.513
the exact same reasons.

00:35:33.513 --> 00:35:35.683
Or if they are, maybe they've done it for,

00:35:35.683 --> 00:35:38.980
there's different ways that
they've been able to achieve that.

00:35:38.980 --> 00:35:42.802
So, again, let the recommenders
speak from their perspective,

00:35:42.802 --> 00:35:47.033
kind of in their words about the things
that stood out about you because that's

00:35:47.033 --> 00:35:49.032
going to be different for all of you.

00:35:49.032 --> 00:35:54.219
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, and
to Allison's point, one of the questions

00:35:54.219 --> 00:35:59.497
that we ask in the letters of reference
is describe the most important

00:35:59.497 --> 00:36:04.327
piece of constructive feedback
you've given the applicant.

00:36:04.327 --> 00:36:08.178
Please detail the circumstances and
the applicant's response.

00:36:08.178 --> 00:36:13.607
As you can tell from this panel, all of us
admissions officers were human, they're

00:36:13.607 --> 00:36:19.200
humans reading your letters, we have
our own flaws and vulnerabilities too.

00:36:19.200 --> 00:36:22.620
And so we know what it's like to make
mistakes and to grow and to get feedback.

00:36:23.750 --> 00:36:27.580
So we don't expect anybody to
be perfect in this process.

00:36:27.580 --> 00:36:30.970
And one of the things we
are interested in is, how do you grow?

00:36:32.100 --> 00:36:34.390
What do you do when you're
giving constructive feedback?

00:36:34.390 --> 00:36:37.282
So we're not expecting
perfection in these letters.

00:36:37.282 --> 00:36:41.430
We're expecting to learn more about you,
and how you contribute to

00:36:41.430 --> 00:36:45.671
the communities and organizations
you're a part of, how you grow.

00:36:45.671 --> 00:36:50.292
Those are the things that we're interested
in learning about in these letters.

00:36:50.292 --> 00:36:54.171
And as you look through some of
the other materials about the letters

00:36:54.171 --> 00:36:57.927
of recommendation,
you'll see exactly what questions we ask.

00:36:57.927 --> 00:37:02.547
So that's something you can do to
prepare and that's another way

00:37:02.547 --> 00:37:07.419
that you can prepare the writers
of your letters of recommendation.

00:37:07.419 --> 00:37:13.735
I wanted to do maybe a lightning round of
what we call tactical practical questions.

00:37:13.735 --> 00:37:18.159
And so these might be a little more fast
paced, but I see that there are a lot of

00:37:18.159 --> 00:37:21.687
questions about word limits and
some of the more tactical and

00:37:21.687 --> 00:37:25.167
practical aspects of writing
letters of recommendation.

00:37:25.167 --> 00:37:30.738
So I want to do a little lightning round
of that if that's cool with our panelists.

00:37:30.738 --> 00:37:35.545
Okay, this is not something I seeded
beforehand, so I just wanted to check.

00:37:35.545 --> 00:37:40.263
So, Katrina has asked a question
that has been uploaded.

00:37:40.263 --> 00:37:45.285
Can I ask whether the word limit for
letters of recommendation is a hard limit?

00:37:45.285 --> 00:37:49.867
I understand that the limit is
usually up to 500 words, but

00:37:49.867 --> 00:37:55.521
I've heard past applicants saying that
the letter of rec is more flexible.

00:37:56.850 --> 00:37:59.396
Lightning response, who wants to go?

00:37:59.396 --> 00:38:02.864
&gt;&gt; Please stay within
the recommended word count.

00:38:02.864 --> 00:38:07.772
We are reading thousands of
letters of recommendation.

00:38:07.772 --> 00:38:12.604
And so the more concise and
specific people can be, the better it is.

00:38:12.604 --> 00:38:16.802
Sometimes the hardest thing is when
you're trying to find that reference or

00:38:16.802 --> 00:38:20.944
that piece of evidence but you're kind
of flipping through a ton of pages.

00:38:20.944 --> 00:38:25.434
So, again, on the side that we're humans
reading it, also be gentle on our eye.

00:38:25.434 --> 00:38:29.539
Sometimes that size eight font
single space can be [LAUGH]

00:38:29.539 --> 00:38:33.393
really tough to really pull
out the media examples.

00:38:33.393 --> 00:38:36.346
&gt;&gt; I mean, we're not sitting
there counting the words.

00:38:36.346 --> 00:38:40.768
So if somebody goes over slightly, we're
not going to know the difference, but

00:38:40.768 --> 00:38:42.047
don't write us a ton.

00:38:42.047 --> 00:38:48.881
And I think some people think more is
better and I wouldn't follow that.

00:38:48.881 --> 00:38:50.885
&gt;&gt; Make it easy for us.

00:38:50.885 --> 00:38:52.491
&gt;&gt; Yeah, make it easy.

00:38:52.491 --> 00:38:55.697
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks y'all.

00:38:55.697 --> 00:39:00.021
Johnny asks, one of my recommenders,
and this is also an uploaded question.

00:39:00.021 --> 00:39:04.201
One of my recommenders will be leaving my
company next month, so he won't have his

00:39:04.201 --> 00:39:08.273
corporate email and phone number,
should I use his personal contact instead?

00:39:13.418 --> 00:39:17.666
&gt;&gt; I believe in that instance you
should contact us, because then

00:39:17.666 --> 00:39:22.487
that way they can verify it and
give you the full instructions for that.

00:39:22.487 --> 00:39:27.941
Nancy in our chat box and Q&amp;A, she is
one of our experts on the process and

00:39:27.941 --> 00:39:32.871
can confirm that, but I believe
that might be the way to go there.

00:39:32.871 --> 00:39:33.588
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:39:33.588 --> 00:39:36.187
&gt;&gt; We want to be able to
reach your recommenders.

00:39:36.187 --> 00:39:39.797
So if their work email is going to
be cut off, I would say yes,

00:39:39.797 --> 00:39:41.501
use their personal email.

00:39:41.501 --> 00:39:45.434
because we want to be able
to reach them if necessary.

00:39:45.434 --> 00:39:46.244
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you all.

00:39:46.244 --> 00:39:51.449
Another upvoted question,
are the recommenders allowed some time

00:39:51.449 --> 00:39:57.306
buffer to submit their forms after
the deadline for applicants has passed?

00:40:00.186 --> 00:40:04.692
&gt;&gt; Similar to what Will already said
about word limit, we have a deadline for

00:40:04.692 --> 00:40:05.352
a reason.

00:40:05.352 --> 00:40:08.882
So we do appreciate if they
try to know obviously,

00:40:08.882 --> 00:40:13.430
especially in places where
connections might be slow or.

00:40:13.430 --> 00:40:16.206
Things like that,
if something does happen,

00:40:16.206 --> 00:40:19.560
and it comes in after reach
out to our desk that we do.

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:24.396
One of our biggest pieces of advice at
the beginning of this was to give your

00:40:24.396 --> 00:40:26.810
recommenders plenty of time.

00:40:26.810 --> 00:40:31.346
And in doing that, you're also letting
them know when the deadline is and

00:40:31.346 --> 00:40:33.478
when the time of that deadline is.

00:40:33.478 --> 00:40:36.750
So hopefully they're not
writing at the last minute and

00:40:36.750 --> 00:40:39.650
frantically trying to
submit at that point.

00:40:39.650 --> 00:40:44.600
So our strongest piece of advice would
be to try to get it in at that deadline.

00:40:44.600 --> 00:40:50.111
But we do know sometimes things can
happen like power outages, internet down.

00:40:50.111 --> 00:40:56.718
Just things that we cannot foresee in that
case well said reach out to our office but

00:40:56.718 --> 00:41:01.813
for the most part we appreciate
if they're in by the deadline.

00:41:01.813 --> 00:41:06.375
&gt;&gt; And I would say if your recommender
is having trouble, have them call or

00:41:06.375 --> 00:41:10.723
email us and at least let us know
in advance that there's a problem.

00:41:10.723 --> 00:41:15.840
And we have tech support who can help them
if they're struggling with the interface.

00:41:15.840 --> 00:41:16.870
&gt;&gt; I have a tip.

00:41:16.870 --> 00:41:21.196
If you're curious about the status of
where your recommend the where that

00:41:21.196 --> 00:41:25.393
letter is, go to my Stanford dot
MBA there's an application tracker.

00:41:25.393 --> 00:41:29.333
So that you'll know once we receive or
once you complete certain parts, and

00:41:29.333 --> 00:41:32.550
that could also help with that
that upward management piece.

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:42.080
&gt;&gt; One last tactical practical question.

00:41:42.080 --> 00:41:47.073
In the how to submit section for letters
of reference where advice to determine

00:41:47.073 --> 00:41:51.620
whether or not you will waive your
rights review each recommendation.

00:41:51.620 --> 00:41:54.060
What should we consider
when making this decision?

00:41:56.990 --> 00:42:00.739
&gt;&gt; Well, the first thing to know
is whether you waive your right or

00:42:00.739 --> 00:42:04.940
not has absolutely no impact on how
we evaluate your recommendation.

00:42:04.940 --> 00:42:10.510
So it has nothing to do with that,
it's more about your right.

00:42:10.510 --> 00:42:14.026
If you are admitted, and
you've come a student here,

00:42:14.026 --> 00:42:16.019
if you have waived your right.

00:42:16.019 --> 00:42:19.191
That means you cannot go
to your student file and

00:42:19.191 --> 00:42:21.660
say I want to see the recommendation.

00:42:23.370 --> 00:42:29.647
And if tell me if I'm saying it wrong,
but if you didn't waive your right,

00:42:29.647 --> 00:42:34.931
then you could go to your student
file once your student here and

00:42:34.931 --> 00:42:38.436
say I'd like to see the recommendation.

00:42:38.436 --> 00:42:41.940
&gt;&gt; I would say think about your
relationship with your recommender.

00:42:41.940 --> 00:42:46.721
Because sometimes if a recommender knows
that you might receive this later on and

00:42:46.721 --> 00:42:50.108
that may skew the things that
they're sharing in there.

00:42:50.108 --> 00:42:55.740
They might not be as open and candid in
the mic, say a little bit more high level.

00:42:55.740 --> 00:43:00.823
So, think about the relationship and
is having that freedom

00:43:00.823 --> 00:43:05.320
of knowing that this is going to
be a safe conversation.

00:43:05.320 --> 00:43:09.200
That they're having with us through
the medium of that letter of reference.

00:43:09.200 --> 00:43:12.110
But they can be that much more open,
truthful.

00:43:12.110 --> 00:43:15.860
And to Alison's point transparent from
the very beginning of the session.

00:43:15.860 --> 00:43:19.325
Let that be one of your guides as
you make the decision of whether or

00:43:19.325 --> 00:43:23.050
not you're willing to waive the right
to see your letter later or not.

00:43:25.100 --> 00:43:28.010
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you all.

00:43:28.010 --> 00:43:32.485
And I just want to reiterate as I think
has come up throughout this session,

00:43:32.485 --> 00:43:34.730
when in doubt just reach out.

00:43:34.730 --> 00:43:38.555
We know that this process, I know I'm
a poet, and I didn't even know it.

00:43:38.555 --> 00:43:40.544
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Then you did it again.

00:43:40.544 --> 00:43:42.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You did it again.

00:43:42.900 --> 00:43:47.752
&gt;&gt; We know that this process can feel so,
foreign to so many people,

00:43:47.752 --> 00:43:51.030
both for applicants and for recommenders.

00:43:51.030 --> 00:43:55.240
So please feel free to reach out to us,
we are here to serve you ultimately.

00:43:56.290 --> 00:44:02.190
So when in doubt, just reach out, and
we read all applications in context.

00:44:03.770 --> 00:44:07.430
We're not expecting this process
to be one size fits all.

00:44:07.430 --> 00:44:11.300
We don't expect people's careers
to be one size fits all.

00:44:11.300 --> 00:44:15.450
So be reassured that we read
applications in context.

00:44:15.450 --> 00:44:20.088
And if there's anything that you
feel like we should know, as you're,

00:44:20.088 --> 00:44:22.521
as we're reading your application,

00:44:22.521 --> 00:44:25.956
you can certainly feel
empowered to let us know that.

00:44:25.956 --> 00:44:30.524
So, recordings of the session
will be available on our YouTube

00:44:30.524 --> 00:44:35.610
channel afterwards, so
you can revisit this conversation later.

00:44:35.610 --> 00:44:39.280
If you need to, you can direct
your letters of recommendation.

00:44:39.280 --> 00:44:46.150
Writers to this video if you need to,
we have about five minutes left.

00:44:46.150 --> 00:44:51.524
So in these five minutes I would
love to hear from our panelists any,

00:44:51.524 --> 00:44:53.421
any parting thoughts.

00:44:53.421 --> 00:44:58.574
Or if there are any myths about
letters of recommendation

00:44:58.574 --> 00:45:02.410
that they would like to miss us right now.

00:45:02.410 --> 00:45:06.220
I would love to hear,
love to hear from our panelists.

00:45:07.420 --> 00:45:11.670
Final, final words of advice or
any myth busting would like to do

00:45:11.670 --> 00:45:14.267
&gt;&gt; Have one mythbuster that Allison

00:45:14.267 --> 00:45:18.330
touched on like the saying
more is not better.

00:45:18.330 --> 00:45:22.192
And so
that was with regards to a word limit.

00:45:22.192 --> 00:45:26.112
But also with the number of letters
of reference [LAUGH] There are some

00:45:26.112 --> 00:45:28.861
rumors out there that it
might be helpful to go,

00:45:28.861 --> 00:45:31.860
above the recommended two
letters of reference.

00:45:31.860 --> 00:45:37.730
And I just wanted to bust that myth
[LAUGH] that more is not better.

00:45:37.730 --> 00:45:40.404
So, there's a reason we asked for two, and

00:45:40.404 --> 00:45:43.526
we always appreciate when
people do keep it at two.

00:45:43.526 --> 00:45:48.295
&gt;&gt; My advice would be, and this isn't
just for the letters of reference,

00:45:48.295 --> 00:45:51.246
this is for the whole application process.

00:45:51.246 --> 00:45:59.310
Try not to worry, and especially worry
over things that you have no control over.

00:45:59.310 --> 00:46:02.030
You don't have any control
over who else applies.

00:46:02.030 --> 00:46:06.290
You don't have any control ultimately
over what your recommender writes.

00:46:06.290 --> 00:46:11.439
So make your best choices and
then just relax and

00:46:11.439 --> 00:46:19.170
forget about it and just don't ain't
Stover over this whole process.

00:46:20.390 --> 00:46:23.701
&gt;&gt; And then I'll do a little
bit more mythbusting,

00:46:23.701 --> 00:46:28.950
word might be out there that you should
have someone from Stanford or with a big

00:46:28.950 --> 00:46:34.155
like famous name writing your letter
because that will help Bust that myth.

00:46:34.155 --> 00:46:36.085
It's not about who's writing the letter.

00:46:36.085 --> 00:46:39.335
It doesn't matter what their
relationship is to Stanford.

00:46:39.335 --> 00:46:42.966
It's what are they writing about
you is that content that evidence,

00:46:42.966 --> 00:46:46.362
and sometimes it's maybe that
manager writes about above you.

00:46:46.362 --> 00:46:49.920
Who's going to be able to write
the most compelling, thoughtful and

00:46:49.920 --> 00:46:52.372
supportive recommendation for you.

00:46:52.372 --> 00:46:55.232
So release that notion and

00:46:55.232 --> 00:47:00.952
trust the people who have
worked the closest video.

00:47:00.952 --> 00:47:04.931
&gt;&gt; Thank you all,
thank you to our panelists for

00:47:04.931 --> 00:47:09.450
your incredible words of advice and
wisdom.

00:47:09.450 --> 00:47:13.724
Thank you to our attendees for your
really, really thoughtful questions and

00:47:13.724 --> 00:47:16.170
for spending a bit of
your day with us today.

00:47:16.170 --> 00:47:18.440
We're really, really grateful for it.

00:47:18.440 --> 00:47:23.360
This application process
is truly a journey.

00:47:23.360 --> 00:47:27.590
&gt;&gt; And we hope that this event is
just one stop along your journey.

00:47:28.590 --> 00:47:31.772
Please continue your journey
at my Stanford dot MBA

00:47:31.772 --> 00:47:36.140
will mention it as a place where
you can track your application.

00:47:36.140 --> 00:47:38.550
Keep track of your letters of reference.

00:47:38.550 --> 00:47:42.958
But it also will help connect you with
opportunities and events to learn more.

00:47:42.958 --> 00:47:47.760
Which we know is so important as you're a
prospective applicant trying to figure out

00:47:47.760 --> 00:47:52.520
whether or not Stanford is right fit for
you and how to navigate this process.

00:47:52.520 --> 00:47:56.160
So please continue your
journey at my Stanford MBA.

00:47:56.160 --> 00:48:00.783
Where you can create a personalized
dashboard, track your application, browse

00:48:00.783 --> 00:48:05.550
courses and clubs, find other events and
connect with student and alumni stories.

00:48:07.180 --> 00:48:10.988
This week we'll continue talking
about the application process, and

00:48:10.988 --> 00:48:12.583
we have many amazing events.

00:48:12.583 --> 00:48:17.531
And we also events throughout the next
couple of months to connect you

00:48:17.531 --> 00:48:22.756
with students and alumni to hear about
what life is like at Stanford GSB.

00:48:22.756 --> 00:48:25.180
And bad, I'll say my last, thank you.

00:48:25.180 --> 00:48:26.580
Thank you so much to our panelists.

00:48:26.580 --> 00:48:30.284
